h its own subject."}}},{"id":"6f95976b-5775-537a-a6d7-56a1a669f189","topicSlug":"reporting-verb-patterns","level":"C1","prompt":"“They admitted ____ the evening meal without permission.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"prepared"},{"id":"B","text":"preparing"},{"id":"C","text":"to prepare"},{"id":"D","text":"prepare"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"After **admit**, this action is expressed by an **-ing clause**.","explanation":"**Admitted preparing** uses an -ing complement for the acknowledged action.","keywords":["admit-gerund"],"skill":"admit-gerund","contextKey":"reporting-verb-patterns-49","fingerprint":"054348c73bded742cf738fa205cfbbdc99693272428b2f19efe945df8c65836e","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-reporting-patterns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["After **admit**, this action is expressed by an **-ing clause**.","**Admitted preparing** uses an -ing complement for the acknowledged action.","Choose **preparing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A simple past form cannot serve as this non-finite complement.","B":"**Admitted preparing** uses an -ing complement for the acknowledged action.","C":"Admit in this construction takes -ing, not a to-infinitive.","D":"A bare base verb does not complete admit here."}}},{"id":"5ae4bfea-0674-5c74-aa53-3272733a11d9","topicSlug":"reporting-verb-patterns","level":"C1","prompt":"“We apologised for ____ the evening meal late.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to prepare"},{"id":"B","text":"prepare"},{"id":"C","text":"prepared"},{"id":"D","text":"preparing"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The key is the **preposition for**, which is followed by a noun or -ing form.","explanation":"After preposition **for**, use **preparing** to name the action being apologised for.","keywords":["apologise-preposition"],"skill":"apologise-preposition","contextKey":"reporting-verb-patterns-49","fingerprint":"afe0fa9c9c3d104e280968cdd4de5c90b79e47082b2c36a15be18806e5f96bf3","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-reporting-patterns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The key is the **preposition for**, which is followed by a noun or -ing form.","After preposition **for**, use **preparing** to name the action being apologised for.","Choose **preparing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A to-infinitive cannot follow for in this action-complement pattern.","B":"A base verb is not the standard complement after for here.","C":"A past form is not the -ing clause required after for.","D":"After preposition **for**, use **preparing** to name the action being apologised for."}}},{"id":"256fedff-3491-5d82-a2a8-883559be1e58","topicSlug":"reporting-verb-patterns","level":"C1","prompt":"“She promised ____ the rowing boat before lunch.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repaired"},{"id":"B","text":"to repair"},{"id":"C","text":"repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"repair"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The reporting verb **promise** takes a **to-infinitive** for the promised action.","explanation":"**Promise to repair** reports her commitment to perform the action.","keywords":["promise-infinitive"],"skill":"promise-infinitive","contextKey":"reporting-verb-patterns-50","fingerprint":"be0533dd639e7126935f336a7d53534048f3ea72761577c701e18d4209f2ad13","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-reporting-patterns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The reporting verb **promise** takes a **to-infinitive** for the promised action.","**Promise to repair** reports her commitment to perform the action.","Choose **to repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A past form cannot serve as the infinitive complement.","B":"**Promise to repair** reports her commitment to perform the action.","C":"Promise does not normally take this bare -ing complement for a commitment.","D":"Promise needs to before the infinitive in this pattern."}}},{"id":"98ab739c-a2a0-5872-a1da-dd79dd4ba9e6","topicSlug":"reporting-verb-patterns","level":"C1","prompt":"“He reminded us ____ the rowing boat before leaving.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repairing"},{"id":"C","text":"repair"},{"id":"D","text":"that repair"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Use **remind + person + to-infinitive**.","explanation":"**Reminded us to repair** names us as the people expected to act.","keywords":["remind-object-infinitive"],"skill":"remind-object-infinitive","contextKey":"reporting-verb-patterns-50","fingerprint":"7e06af573857554d658588239727294ce7818e264156eae5a808b3d4f4c27a80","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-reporting-patterns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Use **remind + person + to-infinitive**.","**Reminded us to repair** names us as the people expected to act.","Choose **to repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**Reminded us to repair** names us as the people expected to act.","B":"Remind + person takes a to-infinitive here, not bare -ing.","C":"The to marker is required in this pattern.","D":"That would need a finite clause with its own subject."}}},{"id":"be4810aa-9a7f-557b-a2b4-8861c5667e69","topicSlug":"reporting-verb-patterns","level":"C1","prompt":"“They admitted ____ the rowing boat without permission.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"After **admit**, this action is expressed by an **-ing clause**.","explanation":"**Admitted repairing** uses an -ing complement for the acknowledged action.","keywords":["admit-gerund"],"skill":"admit-gerund","contextKey":"reporting-verb-patterns-50","fingerprint":"b04be07bf6ac20e477a6abfcee50e74f133e61e7cadf83599f1315f5d66f3d41","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-reporting-patterns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["After **admit**, this action is expressed by an **-ing clause**.","**Admitted repairing** uses an -ing complement for the acknowledged action.","Choose **repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A bare base verb does not complete admit here.","B":"A simple past form cannot serve as this non-finite complement.","C":"**Admitted repairing** uses an -ing complement for the acknowledged action.","D":"Admit in this construction takes -ing, not a to-infinitive."}}},{"id":"51b671fd-fc7c-508c-ac4b-a61c387edd12","topicSlug":"reporting-verb-patterns","level":"C1","prompt":"“We apologised for ____ the rowing boat late.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The key is the **preposition for**, which is followed by a noun or -ing form.","explanation":"After preposition **for**, use **repairing** to name the action being apologised for.","keywords":["apologise-preposition"],"skill":"apologise-preposition","contextKey":"reporting-verb-patterns-50","fingerprint":"7cbbbf4e5a01259f915c456653d927875f8cd5f34b72933e551f64fd1729b54f","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-reporting-patterns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The key is the **preposition for**, which is followed by a noun or -ing form.","After preposition **for**, use **repairing** to name the action being apologised for.","Choose **repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A base verb is not the standard complement after for here.","B":"A past form is not the -ing clause required after for.","C":"After preposition **for**, use **repairing** to name the action being apologised for.","D":"A to-infinitive cannot follow for in this action-complement pattern."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Match the reporting verb to an infinitive, an -ing clause, an object or a preposition.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Verb plus to-infinitive","explanation":"Agree, offer, promise and refuse commonly take a to-infinitive when reporting someone’s intended action. The understood subject of the infinitive is usually the reporting subject. The choice of reporting verb adds information about the speaker’s attitude.","pattern":"She **offered to help**. / They **refused to leave**.","commonMistakes":["Offered helping is not the ordinary pattern when offering to perform an action."],"examples":["“She promised **to prepare** the visitor guide before lunch.”","“She promised **to restore** the old portrait before lunch.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Verb plus object and infinitive","explanation":"Advise, encourage, remind and warn can take an object followed by a to-infinitive. The object names the person expected to do the action. This differs from promise to, where the subject makes a commitment about their own action.","pattern":"He **reminded me to call**.","commonMistakes":["He reminded to call omits the person object required by this pattern."],"examples":["“He reminded us **to prepare** the visitor guide before leaving.”","“He reminded us **to restore** the old portrait before leaving.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Verb plus -ing","explanation":"Admit, deny and suggest can take an -ing clause. They can also have other complements, such as a that-clause. Suggest does not normally use the same object + to-infinitive pattern as advise.","pattern":"She **admitted leaving**. / He **suggested waiting**.","commonMistakes":["Suggested me to wait is not the standard pattern; use suggested that I wait or advised me to wait."],"examples":["“They admitted **preparing** the visitor guide without permission.”","“They admitted **restoring** the old portrait without permission.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Prepositions before -ing","explanation":"Some reporting verbs use a preposition: apologise for, insist on, accuse someone of. A verb after that preposition normally takes -ing. Keep any object required by the reporting verb.","pattern":"They **apologised for arriving** late.","commonMistakes":["Apologised to arrive changes or breaks the pattern; the reason for the apology follows for."],"examples":["“We apologised for **preparing** the visitor guide late.”","“We apologised for **restoring** the old portrait late.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["She **offered to help**. / They **refused to leave**.","He **reminded me to call**.","She **admitted leaving**. / He **suggested waiting**.","They **apologised for arriving** late."]},"stage":"6 · Advanced control","prerequisites":["advanced-passives"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
